#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
safe-run - starts a heavy job under thermal supervision.

What it does that a plain launch does not:
  1. checks BEFORE starting whether the Mac is already hot (and refuses to start),
  2. runs the job at low priority and background QoS (efficiency cores),
  3. gives it its own process group, so coffee-paladin can pause it together with its children,
  4. registers it in ~/.coffee-paladin/managed/ so the guard knows the job explicitly and
     never has to recognise it by name,
  5. enforces a time budget (6 h by default) - then SIGTERM, and SIGKILL 30 s later,
  6. prints a summary at the end: duration, peak temperature, whether it was ever paused.

Usage:
  safe-run -- ffmpeg -i in.mp4 ...
  safe-run --hours 3 --name scan -- python scan.py 6 1000000
  safe-run --allow-hot -- ...      # start even though the Mac is warm (deliberately)
  safe-run --wait-cool -- ...      # on a hot Mac wait for cooldown instead of refusing (exit 3)
  safe-run --grace 120 -- ...      # seconds between SIGTERM and SIGKILL when cleaning up (default 30)
  safe-run --normal -- ...         # all cores, normal priority (overrides job_cores_mode)
  safe-run --efficiency -- ...     # efficiency cores only (overrides job_cores_mode)
  safe-run --cpu-limit 80 -- ...   # cap the job at 80% duty (overrides job_cpu_percent)
  safe-run --allow-sleep -- ...    # do not hold the Mac awake for this job

Defaults come from ~/.coffee-paladin/config.json: job_cores_mode ("efficiency" or "all")
and job_cpu_percent (95 = full speed with a sliver of headroom; below 100 the whole
process group gets cpulimit-style micro-pauses, so it also works for tools that ignore
thread options). The temperature itself is always the guard's job.

Language: TG_LANG=en|pl, or "lang" in ~/.coffee-paladin/config.json. Default: en.
"""

